summary: hierarchy_ref places a node under several parents, with one name and no cycle — so a tag can be a kind of running and a kind of event at once; every tree question reads all its paths.

A `tree_ref` gives a node one parent ([[One position per forest]]). That is right for
a file, which has one path, and wrong for a concept that is a kind of two things: a
marathon is a race (`sport/courir`) ''and'' an event (`évènement`). Neither tree
alone says it, and a tag that is "A and B" is not always a conjunction a query could
write — a marathon is more specific than "a race that is an event", and it may have
narrower tags of its own, which are races and events too.

A `hierarchy_ref` is the type for that: a hierarchy where a node may hang under
several parents. `mfr_path` stays a `tree_ref`; a tag's `path` is a `hierarchy_ref`
in a repository created since October 2026 ([[Tag entries and references]]). Two
types for two meanings, rather than several positions in a forest, whose problems
([[One position per forest]]) are those of a node with one path, not of an
implication. Decided with the user in October 2026.

!! The value

* ''One row per parent'', all bearing the node's ''one name''; a node with a root row (no parent) is a root. The same concept cannot be named differently in two places: a write giving a node two names is refused (`400`), and renaming it is a set of all its rows.
* Adding a parent is an append, leaving one a removal of that row, moving a node a set of its rows.
* `tree_ref`'s JSON shape per row, under its own type: `{"type": "hierarchy_ref", "value": {"parent": <uuid or null>, "name": …}}`, written `path:hierarchy_ref=<parent>/<name>` on the command line ([[Field specifications]]).

!! What stays true

* ''No cycle'': a parent that is the node itself, or below it by any path, is refused. Depth ≤ 1000, as in a forest.
* ''Referential integrity'': a parent is a node of the same hierarchy; a node with children may lose a parent but not its last position ([[Referential integrity of a forest]]).
* ''Names are unique among the children of each parent'', so a path names at most one node.

!! Paths

A node has one path per way up to a root: the marathon is `sport/courir/maraton` and
`évènement/maraton`.

* `path = "évènement/maraton"` resolves segment by segment, as in a tree, and finds it.
* `:path`, `:parent`, `osm(path, …)` read ''every'' path: a node matches when one of them does. `resolve-tree` returns them all, up to 256 a node.
* The clients show every path: one line each in `mf … field get --resolve` and `mf tag list`, in the GUI's detail panel, the node's name once and a line per parent, each naming the parent by its path ([[metarecord-detail panel]]).
* Sorted by path, a node takes the smallest of its positions' keys, each made of the name under its parent's key — the rule of a multi-valued field ([[Pagination and sorting]]).

!! Following

`field ->* …` and `field =>* …` read a hierarchy ([[Following references]]): the
descendants of a node are everything below it by any path, counted once.
`tag -> (path =>* "évènement")` finds what is tagged with a marathon. So subsumption,
the tag queries (`#=évènement`) and the [[path-ref panel]] get the second parent from
the type, with no convention of their own.

!! In the store

Each node keeps the bitmap of its descendants, as in a forest
([[The forest in the store]]), so a subtree stays one read.

* ''Adding'' an edge N → P adds N and N's descendants to P and to each of P's ancestors, by every path up.
* ''Removing'' an edge cannot subtract: an ancestor may still reach those nodes another way. P's bitmap and its ancestors' are recomputed from their children's, the children outside that set standing for their subtrees by their stored bitmaps. The cost is the number of those ancestors, not the depth — negligible for a vocabulary of tags, and the reason `mfr_path` does not use this type.

`tests/hierarchy_ref.rs` holds every bitmap to a walk of the rows after random
sequences of new nodes, added parents, moves and removed parents, and to a rebuild
([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]); `tests/hierarchy_query.rs` holds the
serving path to [[The query oracle]] on every tree question.

!! Tags

`mf tag` reads a tag's ancestors and descendants through all its paths: the string
ancestors of every path of a tag are its ancestors by every parent. Subsumption and
the contradictions keep their meaning ([[Tag subsumption]]): having the marathon
drops the event and the race tags, not being an event takes back the marathon
whichever path named it. `gui-tag-next.sh` sets a tag aside once any of its paths is
under a negative. Exclusivity was removed first: siblings are ambiguous once a node
has several parents ([[Why tags have no exclusivity]]).

A repository whose vocabulary is still a forest keeps it, and `mf tag` keeps writing
`tree_ref` there; [[Converting a tag vocabulary to a hierarchy]] says how to change it.

!! Sync

A `hierarchy_ref` crosses repositories as a `tree_ref` does
([[Ref translation during sync]]): its parent is translated, its name kept. A node is
matched by any of its paths ([[Matching records across repositories]]); a path the
other side lacks is a parent to add there, not a position the record would be forced
out of. On a first sync two different sets of parents are a conflict, settled like
any other ([[Sync conflicts]]).
